---
description: NestJS Backend Rules
globs:
alwaysApply: true
---

### Project Stack
- NestJS with TypeScript (strict mode)
- Prisma ORM for all database operations
- Zod or class-validator + class-transformer for validation
- Winston Logger from src/common/logger
- PNPM as package manager
- JWT for authentication
- Passport.js for auth strategies

### Strict Type Safety & Best Practices
- **No `any`**: Never use the `any` type. Use specific interfaces, types, or `unknown`.
- **Dependency Injection**: When extending base classes (e.g., `BasePaginationService`), always inject required dependencies in the child class and pass them to `super()`. Never use `super(null as any)`.
- **Async/Await**: Every `async` method must contain at least one `await`. If a method only returns a promise, return it directly and remove the `async` keyword.
- **Prisma Types**: 
  - Import models directly from `@prisma/client` (e.g., `import { User } from '@prisma/client'`).
  - Avoid `Prisma.ModelGetPayload<{}>` and the unsafe `{}` type.
  - Use `Prisma.ModelGetPayload<{ include: { ... } }>` only for complex relations, otherwise prefer the direct model type.

### Architecture
- Follow Clean Architecture principles:
  Controller → Use Case → Service → Repository → Database
- Strict separation of layers — no cross-layer imports
- Each module must be self-contained (NestJS module pattern)
- Dependency injection for all services and repositories
- Domain logic must never depend on frameworks or infrastructure

### Module Structure
Each feature module must follow this structure:
  feature/
  ├── controllers/        # HTTP handlers only — no business logic
  ├── use-cases/          # One class per business operation
  ├── services/           # Domain services, complex logic
  ├── repositories/       # Prisma queries, data access
  ├── dto/                # Request/Response DTOs
  ├── interfaces/         # TypeScript interfaces
  ├── entities/           # Domain entities
  └── feature.module.ts   # NestJS module declaration

### File Naming Convention
| File | When to use |
|---|---|
| *.controller.ts | Route handlers, HTTP layer only |
| *.use-case.ts | Single business operation (CreateUserUseCase) |
| *.service.ts | Domain logic shared between use-cases |
| *.repository.ts | Prisma queries, data source abstraction |
| *.dto.ts | Request/Response Data Transfer Objects |
| *.entity.ts | Domain objects with business logic |
| *.interface.ts | TypeScript contracts and abstractions |
| *.types.ts | Enums, utility types |
| *.schema.ts | Zod validation schemas |
| *.constants.ts | Fixed values, config constants |
| *.guard.ts | NestJS guards (auth, roles) |
| *.decorator.ts | Custom decorators |
| *.pipe.ts | Validation/transformation pipes |
| *.interceptor.ts | Logging, transform response interceptors |
| *.filter.ts | Global exception filters |
| *.config.ts | Module configuration |
| *.factory.ts | Object/data generation factories |

### Controllers
- Controllers handle HTTP only — no business logic allowed
- One controller per resource (UsersController, AuthController)
- Always use typed DTOs for request body and response
- Always use appropriate HTTP decorators (@Get, @Post, @Put, @Delete, @Patch)
- Document all endpoints with Swagger (@ApiTags, @ApiOperation, @ApiResponse)
- Use guards for protected routes (@UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard))
- Example:
  @Post()
  @ApiOperation({ summary: 'Create user' })
  async create(@Body() dto: CreateUserDto): Promise<UserResponseDto> {
    return this.createUserUseCase.execute(dto)
  }

### Use Cases
- One class = one business operation (Single Responsibility)
- Name: VerbNounUseCase (CreateUserUseCase, DeletePostUseCase)
- Must have a single public method: execute(dto): Promise<Result>
- Use Cases orchestrate services and repositories — no direct Prisma calls
- Example:
  @Injectable()
  export class CreateUserUseCase {
    constructor(private readonly userRepository: UserRepository) {}
    async execute(dto: CreateUserDto): Promise<UserEntity> { ... }
  }

### Repositories
- All database queries go through repositories only — never Prisma in controllers/use-cases
- Always use Prisma ORM — no raw SQL unless absolutely necessary
- Abstract repositories behind interfaces for testability:
  abstract class IUserRepository { abstract findById(id: string): Promise<UserEntity | null> }
- Use Prisma transactions for multi-step operations:
  prisma.$transaction([...])
- Always handle Prisma errors and map them to domain errors

### DTOs
- Use class-validator decorators for all input DTOs:
  @IsString() @IsEmail() @IsNotEmpty() @MinLength(8)
- Use class-transformer for serialization:
  @Exclude() for sensitive fields (password, tokens)
  @Expose() for explicitly allowed fields
- Separate Request DTOs and Response DTOs
- Never return raw Prisma entities from controllers

### Validation
- Apply global ValidationPipe in main.ts:
  app.useGlobalPipes(new ValidationPipe({ whitelist: true, forbidNonWhitelisted: true, transform: true }))
- whitelist: true — strips unknown properties automatically
- forbidNonWhitelisted: true — throws on unknown properties
- transform: true — auto-transforms primitives to correct types
- Validate all env variables at startup with Joi or Zod

### Error Handling
- Use NestJS built-in exceptions: NotFoundException, BadRequestException, UnauthorizedException, ForbiddenException
- Create a global exception filter for unified error response shape:
  { statusCode, message, code, timestamp, path }
- Never expose internal error details or stack traces in production
- Use typed Result pattern in use-cases:
  type Result<T> = { success: true; data: T } | { success: false; error: AppError }
- Map Prisma errors to domain exceptions (P2002 → ConflictException)

### Authentication & Authorization
- Use JWT access + refresh token strategy
- Access token: short TTL (15min), Refresh token: long TTL (7d), stored httpOnly cookie
- Protect all private routes with JwtAuthGuard
- Use RBAC via custom RolesGuard + @Roles() decorator
- Never store plain text passwords — always bcrypt (salt rounds >= 12)
- Rotate refresh tokens on each use (refresh token rotation)
- Invalidate all tokens on logout (token blacklist or DB flag)

### Security
- Apply Helmet middleware: app.use(helmet())
- Apply rate limiting: ThrottlerModule (e.g. 100 req/min per IP)
- Enable CORS with explicit whitelist of allowed origins
- Validate all inputs — never trust client data
- Use Prisma parameterized queries always — never string-concatenated SQL
- Never log sensitive data (passwords, tokens, PII)
- Apply @UseInterceptors(ClassSerializerInterceptor) globally to prevent data leaks
- Regularly audit dependencies: pnpm audit
- Use CSRF protection if using cookie-based auth

### Environment Variables
- All env vars validated at startup via Joi/Zod in src/config/env.validation.ts
- Access env only through ConfigService — never process.env directly:
  constructor(private readonly config: ConfigService) {}
  this.config.get<string>('DATABASE_URL')
- Never commit .env files — use .env.example with all required keys

### Logging
- Always use Winston logger — never use console.log in production:
  import { logger } from 'src/common/logger'
  logger.info('User created', { userId, email })
  logger.error('Database error', { error, context: 'UserRepository' })
- Log all incoming requests via LoggingInterceptor
- Log all unhandled exceptions via GlobalExceptionFilter
- Log levels: error / warn / info / debug
- Never log passwords, tokens, or PII
- Use function Console only in Development envirement
  Console.log("только в dev");
  Console.warn("предупреждение");
  Console.error("ошибка");

### Performance
- Use pagination for all list endpoints (cursor or offset-based)
- Use Prisma select to fetch only required fields — never select *
- Use database indexes for frequently queried fields
- Use NestJS caching (@CacheKey, @CacheTTL) for expensive read operations
- Use Bull/BullMQ for heavy background tasks (emails, exports, processing)
- Prefer async/await — avoid blocking operations in event loop

### API Design
- Follow RESTful conventions:
  GET /users — list, GET /users/:id — single
  POST /users — create, PUT /users/:id — full update
  PATCH /users/:id — partial update, DELETE /users/:id — delete
- API versioning: /api/v1/...
- All responses must follow consistent shape:
  { data, meta?, message?, statusCode }
- Use Swagger for full API documentation (SwaggerModule.setup)
- Use @ApiProperty() on all DTO fields

### Code Quality
- All comments must be in English
- Add JSDoc comments for all public methods and classes
- Document complex business logic and algorithms
- No ESLint rule disabling (no eslint-disable comments)
- No TypeScript any — use unknown with type guards
- Prefer const over let; never use var
- Use path aliases: @/ = src/

### Testing TEMPORARY OFF, dont think about it.
- Unit tests for use-cases and services (Jest)
- Integration tests for repositories with test database
- E2E tests for critical API flows (Supertest + Jest)
- Test files: *.spec.ts placed next to the module
- Mock all external dependencies in unit tests
- Minimum coverage for business logic: 80%
- Use in-memory SQLite or test containers for DB tests

### Documentation
- Store all .md documentation files in /docs folder
- Do not create separate docs for minor changes
- Create a prompts.md file in /docs with all task history
- Update documentation after every significant feature
- Document all public APIs, DTOs and use-cases